WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:04.170
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy and Tony Award-winning

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.805
actress you know from her work

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.807
on "The Good Fight,"
"The Good Wife,"

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.075
and "The Big Bang Theory."

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:10.677
She stars in "The Gilded Age,"

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:12.812
which is available
now on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.414
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:21.220 align:start size:34% position:66% line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.624
[ Laughter in distance ]

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:27.794
[ Indistinct conversations ]

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:30.830
-How can I refuse?

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.332
-Do my eyes deceive me?

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:35.468
-Well, you see...

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:38.804
-Aunt Agnes. What a surprise.

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:41.273
Mrs. Russell never said you
were coming.

00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:43.876
She's just looked in to pay her
compliments to Mr. McAllister,

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:45.178
haven't you, Aunt Agnes?

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:46.545
-Mrs. Van Rhijn.

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:50.016
-I think you know everyone.
You remember Mr. Riggs?

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:51.584
-How could I forget him?

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:52.952
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:55.488
Christine Baranski, everyone.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:58.491
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:05.364 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:12.204 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:15.107
Welcome back.

00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:16.475
-Thank you, everybody.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.711
-I am so happy to have you.
I believe the last time

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.046
you were here,
there was no audience.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:23.316
And you are a woman
who deserves an audience.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:26.619
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.322
-I remember making
a rather splashy bow.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:32.325
-Yeah. You kind of brought
the energy

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.026
of a full house
with your entrance.

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:35.295
-I preferred this time

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:38.464
just humility and a kind of
graceful, easy entrance.

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.233
Yeah. I will tell you this.
I hope you weren't humble

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.435
about the way you looked
on your 70th birthday.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:45.237
You had quite a celebration
on your 70th birthday.

00:01:45.237 --> 00:01:49.041
You were at the Met Ball
looking like a million dollars.

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:50.109
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.310
Look at you!

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.913
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:57.549
-My daughter told me
I look like a mafia boss.

00:01:57.549 --> 00:02:00.519
I thought.
-That is very true.

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:02.221
Here you are with your costar

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:03.923
from "the Gilded Age,"
Luisa Jacobson.

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:07.226
-Oh, beautiful.
-And I actually sat next to her

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:10.730
and it was very hard
to move my chair

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.631
based on her dress.

00:02:12.631 --> 00:02:13.766
-I would think the men, yeah --

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.903
the men had that problem,
stepping on it.

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:17.904
-This was a dress
that she sort of

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:19.405
had to keep in her lap.

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.108
-To say nothing of waiting in
line to have your picture taken

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.476
and stepping on people.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.977
There's a lot of waiting in
line,

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:27.380
and sometimes you are in line
next to people

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.414
you're very happy to see --

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:29.749
maybe someone you're a fan of,

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.151
maybe someone
you're lucky enough to know.

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:36.822
Now I want to ask
if you and Elon Musk are friends

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:39.993
based on the way
you're looking at him.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.194
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.194 --> 00:02:44.697
Is this --

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:48.601
Is this a
"Hello, old chum," situation?

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.370
-I did not meet Elon Musk
and I have no memory

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.073
of turning around and glaring
at him.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:57.043
But it was a sincere glare.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:03:02.015
And my my daughter gave me
that as a mother's Day present.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:04.484
Like, "Oh, look,
there's grandma glaring

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:06.519
at the world's richest man."

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.654
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.654 --> 00:03:10.522
We saw a clip of your show,
because you are --

00:03:10.522 --> 00:03:12.724
you obviously have
a lot of glares in your arsenal,

00:03:12.724 --> 00:03:14.093
as we know from
"The Gilded Age."

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.662
-Oh.
-You're very --

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:18.731
This is another deliciously
snobby woman you get to play.

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:22.502
-Yeah, she's so -- she's such
a snob that she's really funny.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:24.036
-Yeah.
-Or like that scene,

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.171
where you see her
publicly humiliated,

00:03:26.171 --> 00:03:29.375
when you put yourself
on such a pedestal

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:32.512
and you think you're above
everybody, when you fall,

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:35.781
it's a crash, and people
actually feel sorry for you.

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:38.784
So I think there's something
dear about this,

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:40.620
you know, arrogant woman

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.656
who, you know, she's convinced
of her own value system

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:47.593
and the world is going to
change forever because of money.

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:51.330
Hence the glare I have perfected
at Elon Musk.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:52.931
-Yes.
-Hairy eyeball.

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:54.333
-It's happening all over again.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.936
Well, it is. I mean, I think
she makes a very good point

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:59.705
that, you know, they're
right across from her townhouse

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:04.777
on her quiet neighborhood
on the Upper East Side in 1883.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:08.714
There's the equivalent
of a Trump mansion going up,

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:11.050
with all the noise
and all the all the things

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.886
being brought in
and the idea that people can

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:16.455
just buy their way into society

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:19.358
and change the world
because they have money.

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:21.494
I think she makes
a very keen point.

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:22.661
And here we are.

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:24.063
-Here we are.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:26.699
Now, obviously,
I know, in real life,

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:30.136
you are not this judgmental.
You are not this sneering.

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:33.005
But I heard that Louisa,
your costar,

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:36.775
she is Meryl Streep's daughter.
You know, Meryl Streep via,

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.945
I'm sure, a lot of things,
but "Mamma Mia" is like --

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.646
-Yeah, we became pals
during "Mamma Mia,"

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:43.249
and we both
have raised daughters.

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:46.686
So yeah, I got --
I wrote to Meryl and I said,

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.821
"Your daughter is so lovely

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:52.091
and she's so gifted
and she's so hardworking.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.326
It's just so lovely
to work with her."

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:57.196
And Meryl wrote back,
"She's terrified of you."

00:04:57.196 --> 00:05:00.866
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:03.636
Well, that either means
I'm really doing well

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.639
as the character because
I should be terrifying

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.708
and she should be terrified
of me or I'm really a bitch.

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:11.844
You know?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:15.614
-So she and I had plastic houses
next to each other.

00:05:15.614 --> 00:05:17.049
This was during COVID.
-Oh, right.

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:18.751
-We went back to work.

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:21.253
So the only --
on the soundstage,

00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:24.223
the only way we could
just be safely on a soundstage

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:27.026
is they created
these little plastic houses.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:28.728
And Louise and I
were next to each other,

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:30.796
so I would slip her
some chocolates

00:05:30.796 --> 00:05:33.332
underneath the plastic
and, like,

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:38.271
hope that she would, you know,
think that I was a nice person.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:41.573
-Now, I will attest to anyone
who asks,

00:05:41.573 --> 00:05:44.710
my children think
you're a very nice person.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.613
My children are close friends
with your grandchildren.

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:50.182
And what are your -- what do
your grandchildren call you?

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:52.217
What is your name?
-Minnie.

00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:53.586
-Minnie.
-I am Minnie.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:55.621
-It's it's so lovely to hear --
I'm always excited --

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.122
-And your kids call me Minnie.

00:05:57.122 --> 00:05:58.858
They do call you Minnie.
They're very excited to --

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:00.326
-"Ok, when are we going
to Minnie's house?"

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.728
Well, Minnie now has a
plastic trampoline in the water.

00:06:02.728 --> 00:06:04.029
-Yeah.
-So you have to come over

00:06:04.029 --> 00:06:06.065
to Minnie's house.
-Seems like a death trap to me.

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:07.834
No, thank you.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.034
If you can go a whole summer

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:10.603
with nothing
happening to your grandkids,

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:13.739
we will come over the following.
-No.

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:15.607
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:19.278
You're closing the door
on an incredible chapter.

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:21.414
Diane Lockhart,
which is a character you've now

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:24.116
played over 13 seasons
of television

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:26.185
with "The Good Wife"
and then "The Good Fight."

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:28.053
-I know.
-We never could have gone

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:30.389
into the -- certainly gone
into "The Good Wife" thinking

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:32.024
that this would be a character
that they wouldn't spin off

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:33.492
to a show
that's had even a longer run.

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:37.163
-No, I mean, who gets that?
How many performers get 13 years

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:39.131
of playing the same character

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:41.333
on a well-written
television show?

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:43.735
Both shows were well-written,

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:47.039
and we had great casts
and great guest actors.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:50.476
And yeah, playing
a liberal feminist, you know,

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:53.278
from the time Trump was elected,
can you imagine?

00:06:53.278 --> 00:06:56.282
-Yeah, I know.
-Every night, having to --

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:57.450
-[ Laughs ] Right.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.919
I will say, it's nicer
at four years than more.

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:04.256
-Well, post-Trump years
aren't exactly a joy, right?

00:07:04.256 --> 00:07:07.126
-That's true.
He has left an odor.

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:10.095
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:10.095 --> 00:07:14.400
Now, I have to ask, though --
do you know --

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.634
You're shooting it now.

00:07:15.634 --> 00:07:16.935
Have the Kings,
the creators of the show,

00:07:16.935 --> 00:07:18.304
have they told you
how it's going to end?

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:20.005
Are you aware?
-They've given me

00:07:20.005 --> 00:07:23.075
somewhat of an idea,

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.344
but given what we're living
through -- and this show

00:07:25.344 --> 00:07:27.813
is always about what we're
living through in the present,

00:07:27.813 --> 00:07:30.049
so you can imagine it's...

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:33.352
What are the chances
it's going to be a happy ending?

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:35.120
-Well, they're both
a delight to watch.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:36.955
And as always, so nice to have
you here, Minnie.

00:07:36.955 --> 00:07:39.491
Thank you so much
for coming by.

00:07:39.491 --> 00:07:40.960
Christine Baranski, everybody!

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.929
"The Gilded Age" is available
now on HBO and HBO Max.

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:45.731
We'll be right back
with Matthew Goode.

